# env files — never commit these
.env
.env*.local
.env.development.local
.env.test.local
.env.production.local
!.env.local.example

# vercel
.vercel

# figma — raw API responses (~30 MB), regenerated by `npm run figma:pull`.
# The *derived* snapshot in design/ is committed on purpose: this container is
# ephemeral and the API rate limit is inherited across sessions, so a cache that
# does not survive in git is a cache that never gets used. See design/README.md.
.figma-raw/
.figma-cache.json

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo

# editors
.idea
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

# Per-machine Claude Code overrides — holds this machine's MCP connector ids
.claude/settings.local.json

# The places extract. 99 MB and regenerated from Overture in under a minute
# (scripts/places/extract-nl.py), so committing it would be 99 MB of history for
# a file that is never read twice — and GitHub hard-rejects a blob over 100 MB,
# so the next monthly release could turn a `git push` into a wall rather than a
# warning. Both the workflow and the README write it to the repo root.
nl-places.csv

# Python bytecode from scripts/places/.
#
# Keeps it out of the repo, and that is ALL it does — worth saying, because the
# reason it was added invites the opposite reading. `python3 -m py_compile`
# moves the unit-test count from 775 to 776, because no-service-role-key.test.ts
# walks the FILESYSTEM rather than the git index, so it counts the .pyc whether
# or not git can see it. Measured both ways. If that drift ever costs anyone
# real time, the fix is an exclusion in that test, not here.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Isolated git worktrees the Agent tool creates for a subagent. Untracked
# rather than harmless: an unignored worktree reads as a dirty tree, which
# turns every `git status --porcelain` check in the repo — and every review of
# what a branch actually changed — into a false positive.
#
# **That is ALL this line does, and the limit is worth stating** because the
# other half of the problem looks like it should be covered here and is not.
# `eslint.config.mjs` carries a fixed four-entry `globalIgnores` and no
# `includeIgnoreFile`, so ESLint reads none of this file and a bare `eslint`
# descends into a dot-directory happily — a live worktree is a second copy of
# `src/` and doubles `npm run lint`'s output whatever is written here. Remove
# the worktree when the agent is done; ignoring it only keeps the tree clean.
#
# `tsc` is the one that needs no help, for a reason that is easy to get
# backwards: it is not git-awareness but glob expansion — `include`'s
# `**/*.ts[x]` does not descend into a directory whose name starts with `.`,
# so a subagent's half-written file in `.claude/worktrees/` is invisible to
# `npx tsc --noEmit` rather than merely ignored. Measured against a replica of
# this repo's `include`/`exclude`: the same broken file exits 0 under a dotted
# path and TS2322 at the same depth without the dot.
.claude/worktrees/
